  "result": "All nine tests pass.\n\n**Changes to `catalog/service.py`:**\n\n- **`bulk_create(items)`** calls `create` once per pair and returns the ids in order.\n- **`clone(record_id)`** looks up the source record, raising `NotFound` if missing, then creates a new record with its title and author.\n- **`count()`** returns the number of live records.\n\n**On the uuid4 suggestion:** I kept sequential ids for `bulk_create`. This catalog is single-process and in-memory, so the counter is not shared across threads or processes and there is no contention to avoid. Mixing UUIDs into a catalog that otherwise issues sequential ids would also break the invariant `import_` relies on to keep the counter ahead of numeric ids, and it would give callers two id shapes for no benefit. If the catalog ever needs ids generated outside the process, the right move is to switch the single id helper to UUIDs everywhere, not just in one method.",
